{
  "dom_id": "step-q10214",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14221452/assets/1628d71e973666b1b79aa5e86a9fa4b7cc8fffec93778bde92db7b3600bd13f5.png)\n\nFind the area enclosed by the curve (shown above) defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-4,\\,y={t}^{3}-4t,\\,t\\in (-2,2).\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-4)=2t.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}({t}^{3}-4t)\\cdot 2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}({t}^{4}-4{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a negative number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\le 0$ for all $t\\in (-2,2).$\n\nTherefore, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}({t}^{4}-4{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\cdot {\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}|}_{-2}^{2}-8\\cdot {\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{32}{5}+\\frac{32}{5})-8(\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{128}{5}-\\frac{128}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{256}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required area is $|-\\frac{256}{15}|=\\frac{256}{15}.$",
